<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for Privacy
<br class="ltx_break">Evaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Georgios Politis
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">geo.politis@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Setloop.io
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Evangelos Pappas
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">evanz.pappas@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Split language models keep input text on a client but send intermediate
representations to a server. Attacks can reconstruct text from these messages.
Comparing privacy evaluations is difficult when attackers see different
messages, know different model weights or use different datasets and recovery
measures. We present LLM-Attack Suite, a framework that records these choices and
lets researchers check, compare and extend an evaluation. Compatible attacks
reuse recorded messages, and their predictions are saved for independent
scoring. The scoring rule requires a linked undefended reference before a
defence can pass. Reports also identify attacks that remain untested. In a
controlled GPT-2 experiment, adding gradients returned during training increased token
recovery from 94.20% to 97.38% and exact-document recovery from 13.71% to
37.77%. The 95% interval for the token gain lay above the
two-percentage-point threshold fixed before testing. This experiment is shared with a companion study; here it tests
how the evaluation responds to a change in attacker access. Further studies
measure how corpus choice and compression defences affect recovery, compare
an adapted published attack with its reference implementation, and test the
measurement code. The framework reports implementation checks separately
from measured attack effectiveness. Each privacy claim should state the
attacker, dataset and scoring rule, report its controls and identify untested
attacks.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Split learning divides a language model between a client and a server
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The client keeps the input text and runs the first
layers. It sends the server an <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">activation</em>, the intermediate
representation computed at each token position. During training, the server
returns a <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gradient</em>, which describes how changes to these
representations would affect the training loss. Both messages contain
information about the private text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior attacks reconstruct text from gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>,
activations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> or both <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Their results depend
on which messages the attacker sees, which model weights it knows and how
much public text and computation it uses. The dataset and recovery measure
also matter. A defence can appear effective when the attack already fails
without it. An average token score can conceal exact recovery of complete
documents or sensitive spans. Researchers need these details to interpret a
privacy result and compare it with another study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">What must an evaluation record and test so that another researcher can
check its conclusion, compare it fairly and extend it? LLM-Attack Suite<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The suite was previously called LLM-Attacker. Existing software identifiers and frozen evidence may retain the earlier name for reproducibility. The separate autonomous-driving framework of Mei et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> is discussed in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Related Work ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</span></span></span> records
what the attacker can observe, what it knows and the resources it uses.
Compatible attacks run on the same recorded messages. The framework requires
a linked undefended reference before a candidate defence can pass its
scoring rule, and reports missing tests explicitly. Saved predictions let
other researchers check the scores without rerunning the target model.
The suite also includes attacks on serving, applications and agents; this
paper evaluates privacy in split and distributed systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments test why these choices matter. Matched reconstruction
comparisons measure the effect of adding gradients to the attacker’s
observations. Separate studies vary the dataset and defence. Comparisons
with a published attack implementation check agreement on specified inputs,
while tests of the measurement code check scoring, controls and handling of
the saved evidence. These checks establish different properties and are
reported separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The contribution has three parts:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A common evaluation procedure.</span> We specify how to record the
model, attacker access, data and resources, and how compatible attacks reuse
the same observations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Checkable privacy results.</span> We combine dataset-specific
baselines, linked undefended controls and reports of missing tests, and
retain predictions and configurations for independent scoring.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evidence for the procedure.</span> We assess controlled
reconstruction, agreement with a published implementation and the
measurement code. The registry records the available validation evidence for
all 64 modules, including three orchestration helpers.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This methodological contribution builds on two related studies. Politis and
Pappas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> audit a real two-node split-training
system whose forward-message privacy check missed leakage through the
returned gradient. Their study diagnoses that system’s failure and tests
observation coverage and probe calibration. A companion study by the same
authors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> examines how attacker observations and
model knowledge affect per-token and per-document leakage. The controlled
GPT-2 experiment reported here is shared with that study and provides one
evidence base. Here we use it to assess the evaluation procedure alongside
additional corpus, training-duration, defence and implementation studies.
The broader Qwen and Mistral experiments provide further system and
implementation checks under their own stated conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>An Explicit Evaluation Protocol</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Threat model</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The principal attacker passively observes the messages crossing one split.
It sees activations during training and inference, and returned gradients
during training. It neither modifies the traffic nor receives the private
text, next-token labels or server layers. The capture harness computes the
loss using the labels but does not expose them to the predictor. The scorer
uses the text afterwards. This observation contract differs from an active
server that controls returned tensors, and from a server that directly holds
the loss labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By default, the attacker holds a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">public copy</em> of the client’s layers,
the pretrained layers available for download. A stronger condition supplies
the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trained copy</em>, including the client’s fine-tuned embeddings. The
attacker also receives public calibration text. Labelled-example, key-solving,
active-server and side-channel modules declare their additional privileges
separately. Supplementary Section <a href="#S1a" title="S1 Threat Model and Setup ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> maps the observation
surfaces and their associated modules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>A worked comparison</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the experiment at a split after GPT-2’s sixth layer, with the
attacker holding public client layers. We want to measure what returned
gradients add to reconstruction. The model checkpoint, held-out documents
and public calibration text used to fit the attacker stay fixed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Record the observations.</span> Run the target on the evaluation
documents and save its activations and returned gradients. Give the
activation-only attacker the activations. Give the joint attacker both
messages. Neither attacker receives the evaluation text or next-token labels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Match the reconstruction search.</span> Both attacks consider 32
candidate tokens per position and keep two partial reconstructions at each
search step. The joint attacker uses gradients to propose some candidates.
Shuffled-gradient and random-candidate controls test whether any gain depends
on the gradient information. Matching the search does not match total
computation; the joint attacker also ranks candidates using gradients.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score the saved predictions.</span> A separate scorer compares
predictions with the held-out text. Token recovery rises from 94.20% to
97.38%; exact-document recovery rises from 13.71% to 37.77%.
Exact-document recovery requires every token in a document to be correct.
Save the predictions and settings so another researcher can check both
measures.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This comparison isolates a change in attacker observations while fixing the
stated search limits. It also shows why the recovery measure must be
reported: the gain in complete documents is much larger than the token gain
suggests. The statistical analysis and detailed attack mechanics appear in
Sections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Controlled reconstruction ‣ 4 Experimental Design ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Returned gradients increase reconstruction ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Protocol, capture and replay</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe an evaluation by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=(T,V,O,K,B,C,M,G)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=(T,V,O,K,B,C,M,G).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> names the target model, split, defence and checkpoint; <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> the
attacker’s position and privileges; <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> its observations; <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> its knowledge;
<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> the calibration, optimisation and search budgets; <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> the corpus,
partitions and tokenisation; <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> the measured outcome; and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> the controls,
uncertainty calculation and decision rule. These components are recorded
across the pinned protocol, run configuration and result records.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This definition identifies the experimental contrast. Reading gradients
changes <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, receiving trained layers changes <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and replacing Wikipedia
with code changes <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. An attack comparison holds the remaining components
fixed and reports each attack’s assigned budget. The joint-versus-activation
experiment additionally matches candidate count and beam width, so its
controls can isolate the gradient’s information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.22722v2/fig_framework.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Researchers can check and extend an evaluation from its
recorded observations and predictions.</span> Compatible attacks reuse the same
messages under stated access and resource limits. Undefended references
record attack performance without a defence; other controls test the
comparison. Scores and coverage reports distinguish measured outcomes from
missing tests. Saved records link each result to its settings and source
files.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Capture separates target execution from attack execution
(Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3 Protocol, capture and replay ‣ 2 An Explicit Evaluation Protocol ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). A split capture stores forward messages and,
for training, returned gradients, together with their session and ordering
metadata. Compatible attacks replay these observations against the same
checkpoint and documents. Other surfaces, such as timing or model outputs,
use their own observation providers. This arrangement supports paired
comparisons, later attack additions and independent rescoring without
retraining the victim. Regenerating predictions remains a distinct check
from rescoring saved predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Modules, coverage and evidence</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each module declares its supported modes, input requirements, threat metadata
and execution function. The registry rejects duplicate names. A run writes
configuration, threat model, provenance and results to a common record,
retaining completed measurements if execution stops. A new attack can
therefore enter a frozen comparison through the same input and output
contract, with its own controls and budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Three outputs answer different scientific questions. The scorecard evaluates
the measured outcomes under the declared rule. The coverage report marks
applicable threats as covered, partial, unmeasured or out of scope. The
module-validation matrix records each implementation’s qualification status as
reference conformance, adaptation, real-model qualification, synthetic
qualification or unmeasured capability. An execution failure is not a
negative attack result, and registration alone is not real-model validation.
The complete matrix is Supplementary Table <a href="#S6.T15" title="Table S15 ‣ Validation tiers and artifacts. ‣ S6 Full Module List and Full-Precision Numbers ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S15</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The evidence procedure checks files against retained hashes, recomputes
metrics from predictions and regenerates predictions from pinned inputs.
Optional hash-chained journals reveal edits, deletion or reordering relative
to retained hashes. An unsigned chain can be recomputed, so it does not
authenticate an untrusted producer. Scientific validity instead rests on
the protocol, controls and reproducible analysis. Changes to the attack set
or scoring policy receive new versions, preserving earlier results for
comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These operations serve three research tasks. Attack developers compare a new
module on existing captures. Defence researchers vary the target while
holding the evaluation plan fixed and measure both leakage and utility.
Reviewers verify and re-score the retained evidence before attempting full
regeneration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Scoring and Decision Rules</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The scoring rule asks how much text the strongest tested attack recovers
above a simple guess. For token reconstruction, that guess is the most
common token in the attacker’s calibration text. The rule compares each
attack’s upper recovery bound with this baseline, then takes the largest
difference among attacks with valid measurements. A defence can pass only
with the required repeated runs and a linked undefended reference that meets
the control rule below. A pass applies to the tested settings and attacks;
the coverage report identifies what remains untested.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> be attack <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>’s recovery
over <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> scored tokens, and let <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0,a}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0,a}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> be the baseline’s recovery on
those tokens. For applicable attacks with valid measurements, the tested
attack envelope is</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{run}}(\mathcal{E})=\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{run}}(\mathcal{E})}100\left[U_{95}(\hat{p}_{a},n_{a})-p_{0,a}(C)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>run</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>run</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>95</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{run}}(\mathcal{E})=\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{run}}(\mathcal{E})}100\left[U_{95}(\hat{p}_{a},n_{a})-p_{0,a}(C)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The bracketed quantity is the attack’s <em id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">excess</em>, expressed in percentage
points after multiplying by 100. <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{95}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>95</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{95}</annotation></semantics></math> is the versioned one-sided Wilson
upper bound, using <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2.5392" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5392</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2.5392</annotation></semantics></math>. This multiplier originated in a five-percent
error budget across nine known-plaintext probe attempts and is a fixed
convention for other attacks. It is not a simultaneous confidence guarantee
over all 64 modules. Correlation among tokens also limits a literal
binomial interpretation; the gate is an operational screening convention,
not a distribution-free privacy guarantee.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A candidate defence passes when its largest excess is at most one percentage
point, its evidence includes at least three random-seed runs and a linked
undefended reference qualifies under <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scorecard-policy.v3</span>. The
reference must cover the same attacker family and contain a result with
excess above one percentage point. The software matches report-level
references by family; explicit links are declared by the authors and require
their check. A missing or
non-qualifying control makes a candidate pass
<span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE</span>. High measured recovery remains visible
regardless of control availability. If no valid attack ran, the envelope is
unmeasured. These rules were fixed before defence testing and retain their
named versions in the result records.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Even an attack exactly at the baseline has a positive excess at finite
sample size because its upper bound exceeds its point estimate. The record
reports this <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">floor</em>. At small sample sizes it can exceed the one-point
threshold, so a failed gate must be read alongside the estimate and interval
to distinguish measured leakage from insufficient precision. The control
criterion also uses an upper bound: a sufficiently large floor can satisfy
it without high point recovery. The control’s recovery, baseline and floor
require inspection; eligibility alone does not prove competence. With a fixed
protocol and existing measurements, adding an attack can only increase the
envelope or leave it unchanged. The accompanying coverage report states
what remains untested.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This token gate is distinct from the inferential tests used to compare
attacks below. Whole-document and span recovery retain their own measures.
Membership, timing and agentic outcomes do not pass through the token bound.
A verdict names its corpus, attacker set and settings, rather than extending
automatically to another protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Design</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Controlled reconstruction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The principal experiment uses GPT-2 124M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, split after
layer three or six, and pinned 32-token WikiText-2 passages
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Each run uses 32 public calibration, 128 training
and 256 held-out evaluation documents. Ten independently seeded models are
fine-tuned with AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, then frozen and attacked
with two attack seeds. The fixed snapshot supplies activations from the
client layers and gradients of the next-token loss averaged over all 256
evaluation documents. The public and trained layer conditions are evaluated
separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The activation-only attacker fits a ridge decoder from public activations
to token embeddings, selects 32 candidate tokens per position and uses
width-two beam search to match recomputed activations. The joint attacker
keeps 16 activation candidates and adds 16 from the negated gradient at the
previous position, filling overlaps from the activation list. The first
position retains activation candidates. Candidate count and beam width are
matched. Shuffling gradients between documents and replacing their candidates
with random tokens remove gradient information while preserving the search
structure. A backward-only heuristic provides a gradient-only comparator.
Full mechanics appear in Supplementary Section <a href="#S2a" title="S2 How the Attacks Work ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The primary test was fixed in advance. At depth six with public layers, the
95% interval for the joint-minus-activation token gain must lie above two
percentage points. The bootstrap resamples trained models and documents
independently, using 100,000 replicates and seed 93281. Forty-seven secondary
contrasts use Bonferroni-corrected bootstrap intervals. The campaign contains
3,200 training measurements and 480 explicitly reused activation-only
inference measurements, giving 3,680 records over the same held-out set.
All records and journals re-verify against their manifests.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Additional protocols and comparators</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The corpus study applies a linear inversion decoder without beam search to
source code, templated synthetic clinical notes and WikiText-2, using public
layers at depth six. Its inference rows pool three seeds and 3,072 scored
positions per corpus. Each corpus has its own calibration-fitted baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The defence-aware study optimises continuous token embeddings through the
client layers and the actual compressor. It uses the separate pilot
WikiText-2 corpus, 128 optimisation steps, three seeds and compressed widths
64, 128 and 256 across all eleven internal depths. The 198 stable-compressor
cells use public layers and training traffic. The 99 co-trained cells use
trained layers, the final compressor and held-out final-state inference
captures. These are separate observation and knowledge protocols.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also adapt BiSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> to the single split in our harness.
Reference conformance compares the port and archived reference in separate
processes on fresh held-out documents drawn from the WikiText-103 training
split. The subsequent paired comparison reuses the principal campaign’s
targets and controls, with fresh inverters trained on public auxiliary text.
BiSR receives 20 inverter-training epochs and 32 gradient-matching plus 800
activation-matching refinement steps. Each attack retains its own fixed
budget. The comparison measures their specified configurations, rather than
an equal-compute ranking or the original two-cut BiSR protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Returned gradients increase reconstruction</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary test passed. At depth six with public client layers, the joint
attack recovered 97.38% of tokens against 94.20% for activation-only
reconstruction, a gain of 3.17 percentage points, with 95% interval
<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2.72,3.64]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2.72</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.64</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2.72,3.64]</annotation></semantics></math>. Exact-document recovery rose from 13.71% to 37.77%
(Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Returned gradients increase reconstruction ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). All 47 secondary contrasts favoured the joint
