<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Knowing the defence also matters. The validated white-box path recovered
18.85–68.59% mean tokens across the stable-compressor depth profiles,
despite reducing each 768-dimensional activation to 64–256 numbers.
Co-trained bottlenecks showed 23.52–67.68% recovery across their separate
final-state protocol. Each depth mean averages three widths and three seeds.
Twenty checks validated the gradient path, work accounting and capture
matching. All co-trained captures replayed exactly from their saved parts.
The full depth table and budgets are in Supplementary
Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="S3.3 What the validated white-box attack recovers ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Horizon and longer-context studies extend these settings without merging
their protocols. No verdict changed between 20 and 100 training steps in
the six-defence pilot battery. A 500-step extension found that a fixed
random projection suppressed the declared, projection-unaware inversion
attacker below its majority baseline, but also largely prevented useful
learning. Cross-entropy remained 7.42–7.75 against 4.39–4.44 without a
defence. On 64- and 128-token code and Wikipedia passages and 32-token
dialogue, undefended inversion recovery ranged from 76.56% to 90.22%.
Gaussian activation noise with <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=15</annotation></semantics></math> reduced it to 9.96–55.05%.
The supplement retains the individual conditions, controls and utility
measurements rather than combining them into one attack-strength score.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Published-attack conformance and comparison</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our BiSR port and the archived reference produced identical tokens in all
3,072 paired predictions under the single-seam contract. This establishes
implementation conformance on those inputs before comparison with other
attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the adapted experiment, refined BiSR recovered 93.21% at depth six with
public layers, against 97.38% for the joint attack. The paired difference
was <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-4.17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4.17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-4.17</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points, with 95% interval <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-4.79,-3.53]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4.79</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.53</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-4.79,-3.53]</annotation></semantics></math>.
With trained layers, the difference was <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.02</annotation></semantics></math> points, interval
<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-0.63,0.58]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.63</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.58</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-0.63,0.58]</annotation></semantics></math>. Across 15 prespecified secondary contrasts, ten intervals
lay below zero, one above and four crossed zero. The configurations in
Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Published-attack conformance and comparison ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show why the framework retains several attacks
and their budgets rather than naming a universal winner.</p>
</div>
