<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Validation of the measurement procedures</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We selected 25 existing development-test functions before rerunning them
from the reviewed framework revision. Parameterisation yielded 37 cases
covering recovery metrics, score calculations, control eligibility,
observation boundaries, solver fidelity, resampling and evidence validation.
The design combines known-answer fixtures, independent calculations, fault
injection and metamorphic tests of prescribed relationships between outputs
under controlled changes to inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
Inputs comprised constructed sequences and arrays, small randomly initialised
models and synthetic evidence records.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All 37 selected cases matched their expected outcomes, with no failures or
skips. Changing text available only to the BiSR scorer left predictions
byte-identical. Supplying that text to the predictor was rejected. Removing
or weakening a required positive control prevented a candidate pass. In a
dependence stress case, increasing perfectly correlated document columns
from two to 500 preserved the interval <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> instead of treating copies
as independent evidence. Intact evidence was accepted and the specified
corruptions rejected. These fixed-case results establish conformance of the
selected measurement procedures, complementing reference conformance and
real-model experiments. Supplementary Table <a href="#S3.T11" title="Table S11 ‣ S3.8 Validation of the evaluation methodology ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S11</span></a>
reports all seven property groups and their case counts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Cumulative Evidence and Scope</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, the programme establishes consequential leakage under
several independent protocols. The findings include exact structural leakage in a deployed Qwen
split-training system, high text reconstruction in the controlled GPT-2
campaign, and further corpus-, placement-, depth- and defence-dependent
failures. The Qwen audit recovered the real/decoy partition on all 4,096
frames in its main run even though the forward-only privacy gate passed
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Structural recovery and text reconstruction
remain distinct outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The broader qualification programme covers Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B,
Qwen3.5-27B, Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, Mistral-7B-Instruct and
Mistral-NeMo-12B-Instruct in diagnostic, ablation, robustness, obfuscation
and agentic roles. These campaigns record expected, negative and
privacy-breaking outcomes across multiple models and defences. They establish
that modules have been exercised under their declared contracts, alongside
the controlled GPT-2 recovery results. Supplementary
Table <a href="#S3.T10" title="Table S10 ‣ S3.5 What strength is established ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S10</span></a> records each protocol’s threat model,
module, control, model, corpus, budget, outcome, comparator and evidence tier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The live agentic tier illustrates the importance of retaining negative
outcomes. A Qwen3-0.6B planner with reasoning disabled produced measurable
actions in all ten fixed settings and no policy violation in twenty trials,
but blocked all six sanctioned peer-request trials. This qualifies
planner/tool integration and identifies over-blocking. It does not establish
general agentic security. Synthetic qualification, reference conformance
and target-specific security measurements occupy separate tiers throughout
the module matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The strongest quantitative reconstruction comparisons in this paper concern
GPT-2 124M under the stated corpora, lengths and budgets. Their main value
for the framework is that changing a protocol component changes the
conclusion, while retained captures and predictions make that change
inspectable. Corpus-specific baselines, utility measurements and matched
controls supply the interpretation. Aggregating different protocols into a
single success rate would discard it. Longer contexts, new targets and
additional attack budgets can extend the evidence through the same procedure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments used author-controlled systems. Live endpoint modes require
an explicitly supplied host, and the agentic planner accepts local model
targets only.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VFLAIR-LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> combines configurable split inference and fine-tuning
with attacks and defences. garak <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> organises security probing
through generators, probes, detectors and reports, while ML Privacy Meter
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> measures training-data privacy risk through
membership inference. LLM-Attack Suite concentrates on the experimental contract
for split and distributed systems, covering explicit observations and knowledge,
shared captures, linked controls, execution coverage and versioned evidence.
Our comparisons test the consequences of those choices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Mei et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> introduced LLM-Attacker for
LLM-guided closed-loop adversarial scenario generation in autonomous
driving. Our suite was previously released under the same name.
LLM-Attack Suite provides modular attacks against LLM systems, with this
paper focusing on split and distributed privacy evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient reconstruction includes DLG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, TAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and LAMP
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, while activation inversion includes Vec2Text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and
SipIt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. BiSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> combines a learned inverter with
gradient and activation refinement for split fine-tuning. We assess its
reference conformance under the local single-seam contract and compare the
adaptation with fixed-budget alternatives. The remaining modules are local
implementations or adaptations with their evidence tiers stated separately,
not claims of reproducing every cited paper’s complete protocol.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Earlier split-learning attacks demonstrated inversion, model stealing and
label inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Subsequent
work examines why depth alone does not guarantee privacy
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and recovery during split inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
These motivate evaluation across split depths and observation surfaces.
The supplementary catalogue records the contracts and qualification evidence
for membership, side-channel, application and agentic modules. Their endpoints
remain separate from the text-reconstruction gate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-Attack Suite makes privacy evaluation an explicit, reusable experiment.
Its modules share observation contracts and evidence records, its scoring
requires baselines and positive controls, and its coverage report keeps
untested threats visible. The experiments establish that attacker knowledge,
returned gradients, corpus, defence and attack configuration materially
change recovery. Reference conformance and selected measurement-validation
tests support the procedures used to measure those effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Researchers can use the same framework to add attacks to retained captures,
compare defence revisions and give reviewers predictions they can re-score.
Every privacy claim for a split model should therefore name its attacker,
corpus and scoring rule, report the supporting controls and leave untested
attacks labelled as untested.</p>
</div>
</section>
