<section id="S1a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">S1 </span>Threat Model and Setup</h2>

<div id="S1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure S1 ‣ S1 Threat Model and Setup ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> shows the two parties, the traffic between them and where our attacker sits. The <em id="S1a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">client</em> owns the text and runs the first layers, from the token and position embeddings through the block at the split depth. The <em id="S1a.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">server</em> runs the rest: the remaining blocks, the final layer norm and the output projection to vocabulary logits. In training and in inference the client sends the server an activation for each token; during training it also receives back a gradient for each activation, which is the next-token loss differentiated with respect to that activation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.22722v2/fig_system.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/218;" width="476" height="218"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The client keeps the text and its first layers; the attacker reads what crosses the split.</span> Marker A is our attacker, a passive observer of the split traffic. Marker B is the active server, a stronger attacker used only by the active-attack modules.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1a.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our <em id="S1a.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attacker</em> watches this traffic at the split and alters none of it. It can be an eavesdropper on the link or an operator of the server who sees only the tensors that cross the split. It records every activation and, in training, every gradient, and it keeps its own copy of the client’s layers along with a handful of public documents for calibration. The private text and the server’s layers stay out of its reach, and so do the next-token labels, which are the text shifted by one position. For this reason we do not treat the whole server as our attacker, since a server that computed the loss itself would hold those labels. The code that captures the traffic in our experiments runs the server’s layers and computes the loss from the labels, yet it passes neither to the attack; we use the text only to score the attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1a.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A server that alters the tensors or updates it returns, to make the client leak more, is a separate and stronger attacker, the <em id="S1a.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">active server</em>; only the active-attack modules take that role.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1a.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The attacker’s copy of the client’s layers is by default the <em id="S1a.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">public copy</em>, the pretrained model’s first layers as publicly released, which anyone can download. As a stronger condition we give it instead the <em id="S1a.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trained copy</em>, the client’s first layers after training together with the client’s fine-tuned token embeddings. The client does not publish these weights; we give them to the attacker to measure what a leak of them would add, and we count the time to export them in the attack’s cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1a.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Some modules assume a stronger attacker still, and each module states which one it assumes. A <em id="S1a.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">labelled</em> attacker also holds activations whose tokens it knows, either because it chose that text itself or because some source reveals the true token behind an observed activation; we call such a source an <em id="S1a.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">oracle</em>. A <em id="S1a.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">label-free</em> attacker has only public text and public weights. Some defences also multiply every activation by a secret key and replace the key at each <em id="S1a.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">epoch</em>, one full pass over the training data; we call this <em id="S1a.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">secret rotation</em>. A <em id="S1a.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-epoch</em> attacker must start again after each new key, while a <em id="S1a.p5.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-epoch</em> attacker carries information from one key to the next, for example a structure that all keys share or key material leaked in earlier epochs. The key-solving modules model these attackers; the one experiment in this paper that runs them uses no rotation (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="S3.2 Which text the attacker rebuilds ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1a.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The same modules run from three positions without code changes: installed next to the defence, probing a fleet of machines over a network connection, or as a relay on the client-to-server link. The relay forwards both directions unchanged and copies them into a log; once the log reaches its size limit, it stops copying and keeps forwarding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1a.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The activation and the gradient are the first two of the nine observation points in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure S2 ‣ S1 Threat Model and Setup ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>. The other seven are the server’s weights, its outputs, leftover GPU memory, caches and timing, the shape of the traffic, reasoning records returned by an API, and the labels and properties of the data. Apart from the shape of the traffic, these points sit on the server or the client, beyond what an observer at the split can see, so the modules that read them assume a stronger attacker. Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table S1 ‣ S1 Threat Model and Setup ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> lists what crosses each point and the modules that attack it, including five points we added after reviewing the threat model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.22722v2/fig_anatomy.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/206;" width="476" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S2: </span><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An attacker can observe three surfaces on the link, one on the client and five on the server side.</span>
The client holds the private text, the token embeddings and the client layers; the server holds the remaining layers, the final norm and the output layer. Orange boxes are the observation surfaces S1–S9 of the surface model; control-plane metadata, persistent stores and router state extend it. Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table S1 ‣ S1 Threat Model and Setup ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> lists the attacks on each surface. Cutting the model twice, into three parts, adds a second place where activations cross; mixture-of-experts server layers leave the activation unchanged but add routing decisions as a possible leak.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S1.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S1.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">#</span></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S1.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:82.5pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.T1.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Observation point</span></span>
</span></td>
