<section id="S2a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">S2 </span>How the Attacks Work</h2>

<div id="S2a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The strongest reconstruction attack rebuilds a document in four steps. The
activation-only attacker uses steps one, two and four; the joint attacker
adds step three. This attack is a self-contained routine, distinct from the
registered inversion module’s own joint variant, which explores candidates
differently. The four steps match the numbered badges of
Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure S3 ‣ S2 How the Attacks Work ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.22722v2/fig_method.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/158;" width="476" height="158"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S3: </span><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The joint attack takes half of its candidate tokens from the gradient and otherwise runs the same steps as the activation-only attack.</span>
The numbered badges correspond to the four stages described below.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2a.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1a" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1a" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learn a decoder.</span> The attacker sends 32 public documents
through its copy of the client’s layers and records, for each token, the
activation and the token behind it. From these pairs it fits a linear map, a
ridge regression held stable by a penalty on its size, that takes an
activation back to a token embedding, the vector the model gives each word
in its vocabulary.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2a" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidates from the activations.</span> At each position, the
attacker applies this map to the observed activation and lists the 32
tokens whose embeddings have the highest <em id="S2.I1.i2a.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cosine similarity</em> with the
result, meaning they point most nearly in the same direction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3a" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidates from the gradient (joint attack only).</span> The
gradient comes from the next-token loss, whose target at each position is
the document’s next token, so the gradient at one position carries a
signal about the token that follows it. The joint attacker keeps the top 16
candidates from the activation and adds the 16 tokens whose embeddings have
the highest cosine similarity with the negated gradient at the previous
position, filling any overlap from the activation list. No earlier
position predicts the first token, so the first position keeps its
activation candidates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Beam search.</span> A width-two search reads the text left to right,
extending its two leading guesses with each candidate, pushing them back
through the client’s layers, and retaining the two whose recomputed
activations lie closest to what crossed the split. The two attackers try
equally many partial texts and part ways only on their candidate lists.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2a.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2a.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the main experiment the attacker reads one fixed snapshot of this
traffic rather than live training traffic. We fine-tune the whole model, freeze
it, split it, and pass the 256 evaluation documents through it once. The
activations come from the frozen client layers, and the gradients from the
next-token loss averaged over all 256 documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2a.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2a.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2a.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shuffled-gradient</em> control strips the gradient of its information
by giving the joint attacker another document’s gradient in place of the
right one. The <em id="S2a.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">random-candidate</em> control retains the activation half of
the list and draws the rest uniformly from the vocabulary, so no gradient
enters. The <em id="S2a.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backward-only</em> heuristic uses the gradient alone: at each
position it picks the token with the highest cosine similarity to the
negated gradient at the previous position, with no activations and no beam
search. We also run SipIt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, which rebuilds the input
one position at a time: for each position it searches for the token whose
activation, given the prefix already recovered, matches the observed one,
with a budget of steps per position. We implemented the published algorithm
ourselves; when the budget runs out it returns the best token it has tried,
so its guarantee of exact recovery no longer holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2a.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2a.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments also use a <em id="S2a.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">white-box</em>
attack, which knows the defence and searches for continuous token embeddings that,
after passing through the client’s layers and the defence, reproduce the
observed message, then reads off the nearest token at each position.
The <em id="S2a.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inversion attack</em>, a form of vanilla model inversion, fits the
same linear map from activations to embeddings as step one, on its own
calibration text passed through the public layers, and reads off the
nearest token without a beam search. <em id="S2a.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">BiSR</em>, or bidirectional semi-white-box
reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, works in two stages: a learned inverter
first guesses the text from the forward activations, and an optimisation
stage then refines the guess. In our port, the inverter (which the code
names SIP) is a small recurrent network trained on public text passed
through the public layers, and the refinement (which the code names bf)
first matches the observed gradient and then matches the observed
activation. Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table S2 ‣ S2 How the Attacks Work ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a> lists each signal, when it crosses, how
an attack uses it and what it reveals.</p>
</div>
