<section id="S3a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">S3 </span>Detailed Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="S3a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every experiment below is tied to a released machine-readable result and a
hash-checked record. We report each experiment against its prespecified threat
model, controls and statistical procedure, then compare the conclusions across
observation grants, corpora, attack paths and published attacks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.1 </span>What the attacker observes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main experiment fixes its plan before running. It uses 256 held-out
documents per run and ten client models trained with different random
seeds, each attacked twice with different seeds for the attacks’ own random
choices. The primary statistical test fixed in advance is the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two-point
gain test</em>: at depth six with the public copy of the layers, the joint
attacker’s token recovery minus the activation-only attacker’s must have
the lower end of its 95% interval above two percentage points. The
interval comes from a bootstrap that resamples trained models and documents
independently. The protocol also prespecified 47 secondary comparisons, each
tested with a Bonferroni-corrected bootstrap interval. The client’s model is
GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> (124M) on pinned 32-token WikiText-2 passages
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, split after the third or the sixth layer, with 32
public calibration, 128 training and 256 evaluation documents. Training uses
AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. The attacker holds either the public first
layers or the client’s trained first layers. It attacks the fixed snapshot
described in Section <a href="#S2a" title="S2 How the Attacks Work ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>: in training mode it reads the
activation and the gradient, in inference mode the activation alone. In all, 40 attack configurations (28
without a defence, 12 with one) run against ten trained models at both
depths with both copies of the layers, for 3,200 measurements; the six
activation-only configurations are reused for inference, adding 480, for
3,680 in total, each on the same 256 documents. Sixteen slices ran on two
machines with hash-chained logs; the sharding, the merge and the counts are
in Section <a href="#S4a" title="S4 Release and Reproduction ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The gain test passed (Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table S3 ‣ S3.1 What the attacker observes ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a>). At depth six with the
public layers, the joint attacker recovered 97.38% of tokens against
94.20% for the activation-only attacker, 3.17 points more (95% interval
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[+2.72,+3.64]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.72</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.64</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[+2.72,+3.64]</annotation></semantics></math>). The 47 secondary comparisons cover the joint
attacker against the activation-only attacker, the shuffled-gradient
control, the random-candidate control and the backward-only heuristic, at
both depths, with both copies of the layers and with candidate lists of 8,
16 and 32 tokens, leaving out the gain test itself. In all 47 the joint
attacker came out ahead, with intervals above zero after a Bonferroni
correction for the number of comparisons. The backward-only heuristic,
which reads the gradient alone, recovers 49.98% of tokens in the same
setting. In the two controls, the shuffled gradient recovers 93.91% and
the random candidates 93.89%, both about 0.3 points below the 94.20%
activation-only result, while replacing the true gradient with a perturbed
one leaves recovery near the true-gradient result, at 97.31% when we add Gaussian noise
whose standard deviation is 0.1 times the root mean square of each
document’s gradient, and 97.33% when we quantise the gradient to eight
bits. Together, these controls isolate the information contributed by the
returned gradient.</p>
</div>
