<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.2 </span>Which text the attacker rebuilds</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The same attacker recovers very different amounts on different corpora
(Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table S4 ‣ S3.2 Which text the attacker rebuilds ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S4</span></a>). We ran the undefended inversion attack on three
pinned corpora, each scored against its own trivial guess: this repository’s
own Python source code, synthetic clinical-style notes written from
templates, and a WikiText-2 reference. The trivial guess itself differs: it
scores 2.64% on Wikipedia and 20.31% on code, almost eight times higher.
Recovery tracks how regular the text is. The attacker recovers every token
of the templated clinical notes, 64.94% of the code and 63.25% of the
Wikipedia text, so corpus choice alone moves recovery by up to 37 points.
Against the corpus-specific guess rates, excess recovery is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+46.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>46.78</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+46.78</annotation></semantics></math> points
for code, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+93.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>93.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+93.75</annotation></semantics></math> points for the clinical notes and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+62.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>62.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+62.79</annotation></semantics></math> points for
Wikipedia.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finite-sample floors are 2.3–3.9 points for the 768-position training rows
(Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table S5 ‣ S3.2 Which text the attacker rebuilds ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S5</span></a>) and 0.84–1.90 points for the 3,072-position
inference rows. Every undefended result exceeds its corpus-specific guess by
at least 46 points, so every undefended condition fails the privacy gate.
Added Gaussian noise (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math>) changed mean recovery by at most 1.4
points; the paired intervals include zero for code and Wikipedia and are
exactly zero for the templated clinical corpus.</p>
</div>
