<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The machine-readable domain results are
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/domain-shift-v1/domain-shift-v1-summary.json" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink">domain-shift-v1/domain-shift-v1-summary.json</span></a>
and
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/domain-shift-v1/domain-shift-v1-defended-summary.json" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink">domain-shift-v1/domain-shift-v1-defended-summary.json</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluator also chooses how long the client trains. A
long-horizon battery ran six defences at 20 and 100 training steps, over
three training seeds, on the <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pilot corpus</em>: 96 pinned WikiText-2
passages from the dataset’s training split, which we used while building
the framework and which are separate from the main experiment’s held-out
documents (Table <a href="#S3.T6" title="Table S6 ‣ S3.2 Which text the attacker rebuilds ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S6</span></a>). The defences are lightweight stand-ins
for defences in the VFLAIR-LLM collection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, each a plain
transform. <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Added noise</em> adds Gaussian noise with standard deviation
15 to every number of the activation. <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sparsify-10%</em> keeps only the
largest tenth of each activation’s numbers. <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mid-lite</em>, named after
the mutual-information defence but learning nothing, projects the
activation through a fixed random map down to 64 numbers. <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Santext</em>,
named after the SanText text-sanitisation defence, swaps each input token,
with probability 0.3, for one of its nearest tokens in the embedding table.
<em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">At-lite</em>, named after adversarial training but performing none,
shifts every activation by a fixed amount along the direction in which
activations vary most. The corpus’s majority token does not occur in the
evaluated passages, giving the battery a fixed 0% guess rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">No verdict changed between 20 and 100 steps. Against the declared inversion
attacker, which does not know the projection, mid-lite remained at 0%
recovery. The 500-step extension shows that mid-lite achieves this result by
largely preventing learning: mean training cross-entropy is 7.42–7.75 versus
4.39–4.44 without a defence. Added-noise recovery rose from 0.3% in steps
0–7 to 4.2% in steps 50–99, and sparsify-10% rose from 17.5% to 25.9%.
A document-clustered re-analysis changed no verdict, although the 20-step
added-noise interval (<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[+0.47,+1.35]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.47</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.35</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[+0.47,+1.35]</annotation></semantics></math> points) crossed the one-point threshold.</p>
</div>
