and
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/defence-training-500-step-extension/REPRODUCTION.json" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink">defence-training-500-step-extension/REPRODUCTION.json</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.3 </span>What the validated white-box attack recovers</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The white-box analysis optimises the complete differentiable path from token
embeddings through the client’s layers and the true compressor to the observed
message. Twenty PyTorch checks validate the gradient path, work accounting and
capture matching. We evaluated 198 stable-compressor cells across all eleven
internal GPT-2 depths and a separate 99-cell co-trained tier on held-out
final-state inference captures. Each defence compresses the activation.
<em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PCA compression</em> sends only the activation’s top 64, 128 or 256
principal components out of its 768 numbers (the <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">width</em>), fitted once
on calibration text. A <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trained bottleneck</em> starts from the same
projection and adds a small learned correction, trained to defeat probes
that try to read the tokens. Across three training seeds, both methods and
three widths, the attack recovers between 18.85% and 68.59% of tokens on
average at every depth (Table <a href="#S3.T7" title="Table S7 ‣ S3.3 What the validated white-box attack recovers ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S7</span></a>). Each mean averages the
three widths.</p>
</div>
