<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A separate 99-run tier trains the bottleneck alongside the client, then
attacks held-out inference captures from the final state, giving the
attacker the client’s trained layers and the trained compressor; every
capture replays exactly from the saved parts. Like the rest of the sweep,
it uses the pilot corpus. We report the stable and co-trained tiers separately,
with their verified cells, means and work counts in
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/all-boundary-whitebox-reconstruction/full/summary.json" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink">all-boundary-whitebox-reconstruction/full/summary.json</span></a>;
full compute counts and wall times are in Section <a href="#S6a" title="S6 Full Module List and Full-Precision Numbers ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also ran the inversion attack on longer texts and on dialogue
(Table <a href="#S3.T8" title="Table S8 ‣ S3.3 What the validated white-box attack recovers ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S8</span></a>), at depth six over three training seeds with
no defence or added noise, on code and Wikipedia text at 64 and 128 tokens
and on 32-token dialogue prefixes from 96 dialogues. Undefended recovery
ranges from 76.56% to 90.22%; added noise lowers it to 9.96–55.05%.
Each condition uses three training seeds, and the released records include
its majority-guess rate. Noise-aware whitening lowers inference recovery from
21.65% to 13.09% and raises training recovery from 11.46% to 13.67%, so
the plain and whitening variants expose different signals.</p>
</div>
