<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.4 </span>Which published attack the evaluator runs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first checked that our port of BiSR matches the reference. Using the same
saved models, inverters, observations and budgets as an archived copy of the
upstream source, and running our port and the reference in separate
processes on 256 fresh 32-token documents at depths three and six, with
inverters trained under three seeds, both produced identical predicted tokens in all 3,072 paired runs;
every seed-level difference was zero. These documents, and the 112
auxiliary articles on which the inverters were trained, come from the pinned
WikiText-103 training split, with the evaluation documents held out from
it; none comes from the official test split, and the selection excludes the
inputs of earlier runs and the WikiText-2 splits. This establishes conformance
for the single-seam setting used by our harness. BiSR’s published
<em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two-cut setting</em> is a distinct protocol: it cuts GPT-2-large into three
parts and exposes activations at one cut and gradients at the other, whereas
both signals cross the one cut in our harness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then compared the port with our joint attack under a plan fixed before
running (Table <a href="#S3.T9" title="Table S9 ‣ S3.4 Which published attack the evaluator runs ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S9</span></a>). It kept the main experiment’s 256
documents, ten trained models, both depths and both copies of the layers,
reused the earlier results as controls, and trained fresh inverters on the
public auxiliary text. Each attack used its own fixed protocol budget. At depth six
with the public layers, the port recovered 93.21% of tokens against 97.38%
for the joint attack, 4.17 points fewer (95% interval <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-4.79,-3.53]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4.79</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.53</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-4.79,-3.53]</annotation></semantics></math>),
with the port lower in every trained model, and the whole interval lies
more than two points below zero. Across fifteen prespecified secondary
contrasts, ten intervals lie below zero, one above and four cross zero.
Notably, the refined port matches the joint attack at depth six with trained
layers (<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.02</annotation></semantics></math> points, interval <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-0.63,+0.58]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.63</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.58</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-0.63,+0.58]</annotation></semantics></math>), so no attack dominates
every setting.</p>
</div>
