<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cumulative evidence.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, the programme supports the following statement:
LLM-Attack Suite has exposed consequential leakage under several independent
protocols: exact structural leakage in a deployed Qwen split-training system,
high text reconstruction in a controlled GPT-2 campaign, and additional
corpus-, placement-, depth- and defence-dependent failures. Its broader Qwen
and Mistral campaigns provide qualification evidence that different modules
have been exercised against multiple models and defences, including expected,
negative and privacy-breaking outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T15" title="Table S15 ‣ Validation tiers and artifacts. ‣ S6 Full Module List and Full-Precision Numbers ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S15</span></a> accounts for all 64 modules and
records the evidence tier, controls and status of each.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.6 </span>Live agentic qualification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A live tier used Qwen3-0.6B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> as the local planner under
the same sandbox, permission checks, side-effect logging and budgets as the
synthetic tier. With reasoning mode disabled, all ten prespecified settings
produced measurable actions. No policy violation was recorded in the twenty
fixed scenario trials. All three sanctioned peer-request settings were
blocked in both trials, giving six false blocks; the result therefore
establishes successful planner/tool integration and identifies systematic
over-blocking of sanctioned requests. The full measurements and planner traces
are in
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/agentic-nonthinking-decoding/raw/FOLLOWUP.json" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink">agentic-nonthinking-decoding/raw/FOLLOWUP.json</span></a>
and
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/agentic-nonthinking-decoding/raw/planner-traces.jsonl" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink">agentic-nonthinking-decoding/raw/planner-traces.jsonl</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.7 </span>Framework operation across protocols</h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The completed campaigns exercise the workflow in
Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3 Protocol, capture and replay ‣ 2 An Explicit Evaluation Protocol ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> at several stages. The main campaign reused
captures across attack configurations and assembled 3,680 measurements,
including 480 explicitly reused inference measurements, from sixteen
shards. Its
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/v3-campaign/merge-verification.json" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">merge-verification record</a>
and
<a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Setloop/LLM-Attack-Suite/blob/v2.0/v3-campaign/EVIDENCE-RELEASE.md" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">release and re-verification guide</a>
document the assembly and provenance checks. The BiSR conformance study
tests implementation fidelity against a reference executable; its adapted
comparison demonstrates how another attack can join a frozen experiment.
The white-box study exercises defence-aware reconstruction across internal
split depths, and the live agentic study connects a local planner to the
sandbox’s action and policy records.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Qwen and Mistral campaigns extend module qualification across the models
and defences recorded in Table <a href="#S6.T15" title="Table S15 ‣ Validation tiers and artifacts. ‣ S6 Full Module List and Full-Precision Numbers ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S15</span></a>. Together, these
studies establish reuse of the framework across reconstruction, robustness,
obfuscation and agentic protocols. Their shared methodological role is to
connect an explicit attacker contract to controls, measured outcomes and
retained evidence. This is the basis for adding a new attack or target to
the programme while preserving the interpretation of earlier results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.8 </span>Validation of the evaluation methodology</h3>

<div id="S3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We tested whether the evaluation code preserves the intended meaning of
recovery measurements and rejects specified errors in their supporting
evidence. The design combines known-answer fixtures, independent numerical
calculations and fault injection with metamorphic tests: checks of prescribed
relationships between outputs under controlled changes to the inputs
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. For example, changing text available only to the
scorer must leave the attack’s predictions unchanged, while removing a
required positive control must prevent a candidate pass.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We fixed a selection of 25 existing development-test functions before
re-executing them from the reviewed framework revision on CPU, retaining
their original assertions and numerical tolerances. Parameterised inputs
expanded the selection to 37 cases across seven measurement properties
(Table <a href="#S3.T11" title="Table S11 ‣ S3.8 Validation of the evaluation methodology ‣ S3 Detailed Experimental Results ‣ LLM-Attack Suite: A Unified Red-Team Framework for PrivacyEvaluation of Split and Distributed LLM Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S11</span></a>). The inputs were constructed
token sequences, numerical arrays, small randomly initialised models and
synthetic evidence records.</p>
</div>
